  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "f1",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "lint.name-comparison"
      ],
      "text": "name_comparison is a parameter of lint, yet term marks are also resolved outside lint: the impact search through terms (lint.references Rationale, system Goal \"resolving terms\") must find the same entry for a mark as lint does. No system-level element says that every capability resolving a term mark compares names this way, and other capabilities cannot see lint's parameters.",
      "status": "dismissed",
      "reason": "Only lint compares names; baseline follows the entry a mark refers to, as lint.references.term decides."
    },
    {
      "id": "f2",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "lint.name-comparison"
      ],
      "text": "Step 2 drops \"any white space at either end, a non-breaking space included\", but nothing says which characters count as white space (for example Unicode's White_Space property, or also zero-width characters such as U+200B or U+FEFF). That decision is left to whichever library the code uses.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in 664d4a4"
    },
    {
      "id": "f3",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "lint.references.reference-ambiguous",
        "lint.references.reference"
      ],
      "text": "The Rationale of name_comparison claims \"one comparison for every name\", but its body covers only glossary names, aliases, term marks and text matches. No rule says how the names of contract elements and parameters compare in lint.references.reference-ambiguous (\"has <name>\") and lint.references.reference: exactly, or as name_comparison says.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in 664d4a4"
    },
    {
      "id": "f4",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "lint.name-comparison"
      ],
      "text": "Step 3 joins \"each run of spaces and tabs between two words\", but name_comparison does not say what a word is. The only definition in the capability is the one for matching in lint.references.marked: a run of letters, combining marks, digits, hyphens and underscores. Read that way, a run of spaces next to any other character, as in a name like \"C++  code\" or \"U.S.  law\", is not between two words and is not collapsed. Read loosely, it is. The tree does not decide which.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in 8997b8b"
    },
    {
      "id": "f5",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "lint.name-comparison",
        "lint.references.term"
      ],
      "text": "name_comparison is a parameter of lint, so other capabilities cannot see it, yet the system's Scope says impact sets and context packs follow term marks. Nothing at system level says those capabilities compare names the same way. A mark lint accepts, such as [Spec Tree] or one written decomposed, could then be missed by the impact search through terms, the very loss the References Rationale guards against.",
      "status": "dismissed",
      "reason": "A repeat of f1: only lint compares names; impact and pack follow the entry lint resolved."
    },
    {
      "id": "f6",
      "kind": "contradiction",
      "elements": [
        "lint.name-comparison",
        "lint.references.term"
      ],
      "text": "The prose of name_comparison says 'Only spaces and tabs join words, so that no other character makes two words one name'. Yet lint.references.term's example 'U.S.  law' resolves to an entry whose name joins the words U and S with '.', and '.' ends a word as lint.references.marked defines it. Read literally, the sentence forbids names with punctuation between words, which the example accepts.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in 018cd88"
    },
    {
      "id": "f7",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "lint.name-comparison"
      ],
      "text": "Step 4, lower case, comes after step 1, NFC, so lower case can leave a name that is no longer in NFC. 'J' followed by a combining caron becomes 'j' followed by the caron, while the entry 'ǰ' stays U+01F0. The two names differ only in case and still do not compare the same. The tree does not say whether that outcome is intended.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in 018cd88"
    }
  ]
